A||N||10||bHooper||More questions about engine conversions||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||06-01-2000||11:14 AM||||So now that I have the GT moving around town,  and close to be street legal, I am thinking about my next project... a engine conversion.<P>Therefore, I have some questions about the various engines (v-6) that people have used/considered/installing or dreamed about.<P>1st, there is the 3.4 liter camero/firebird engine.  As I understand it this is the most popular, and easiest, upgrade.  It requries moving the starter, modifing the cradle mounts (in what way i do not know), using the 3.4 injectors (or a fuel regulator) and if desired, swaping the plenum and emisions thingys from the 2.8 liter.  It also requires  using the 3.1 liter flywheel (or the 88 fiero gt one) and the 2.8's headers as well as the 3.4's computer and wireing harness.  <P>Is this about correct?  Will the camero computer work with the getrag tranny?  Or will I need to keep the original Fiero ECM?  <P>From what I understand, one should expect around 160 hp and 185-200 ftlbs of torque.  Wouldn't this number be a slightly lower useing the Fiero ECM?  Or is that with the Fiero ECM?<P>2nd, I have not found as much info on the GM DHC 3.4 engine, used in the later model camero/firebird.  From what I have found, this engine produces 210 hp and 215 ftlbs of torque.<P>What is required to swap this engine into a Fiero?  Is a kit necessary?  Or has this engine been deemed to expensive of a option, and everyone has gone with the MPI version or  the Buick 3800?... which brings me to my 3rd and final engine option.<P>3rd, the Buick 3800.  This engine also comes in the SC configuration.  It is obviously a desirable switch, but costly (in either version) and requires a kit to install.  I do not think this will fit my needs and budget, like the 3.4 liter engines... unless told differently  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I have no desire to force a v-8 into the car, as I do not want the excesive weight gain... nor do I want to cut or remove any portion of the car.  I am interested in haveing the best torque and horsepower gain's for the money (and time), as I plan on keeping this car as a second car and a part time SCCA racer.  <P>As for the suspension, there is a local suspension builder (used to build IMSA racecars) that I plan on talking to about removing everything and building a complete and custom suspension (not as expensive as it sounds... he did the same to a BMW 325 for under $700!).  And as much as I would like to have that first, I know it is best to have the engine swap done b4, so as to allow for any weight differintial in the car.<P>Thanks for reading my rambling thoughts, and imputing any that you might have.  As everyone knows, I am not a mechanic... just a guy that wants to have a nice sportcar without having to shell out the cash for a new one. However, I am willing and interested in doing as much as I can, myself!<P>Thanks again!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||06-01-2000||11:50 AM||||Hoop...<BR>The Camaro 3.4 swap uses the Fiero ECM. Some folks change proms, but I believe the stock one will work okay.<P>I got a ride in a Camaro 3.4 conversion. It was *quick*. <BR>The owner estimated the HP at 180. This is probably realistic, due to the shorter exhaust system, the FI, and the lack of a power steering pump. The 160 HP rating is for an S-10 with a carb or a TBI.<P>The TDC 3.4 came in the Monte Carlo and Grand Prix. Not in the RWD Camaro or Firebird.<P>I have heard that the 3.4 TDC is a "lot of fun" to install, and sort out. <BR>They are also fairly uncommon. (read: expensive) <P>The 3800 swap requires a bit of fabrication, but the 3800 engines are everywhere, and they are as reliable as an anvil. <P>I haven't decided what I'm gonna do with mine yet. Probably a Camaro 3.4 in the Formula, unless I sell it. <BR>Or a 3800 in my 88 coupe.<P>Whichever option throws itself in my direction. (Probably just drive 'em, for a while. I'm lazy as hell.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Just my $.02.<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||06-01-2000||03:07 PM||||I plan on swapping motors either next summer or the summer after.  I hope to have a few thousand bucks tucked away, but not nearly enough for a 3800SC swap (I wish!).  Anyone know about what the cost of the 3.4 would be (including installation)?  I know that Ed Parks (??) does them down in Alabama but I forget his ##.  I can't really do any of the work myself as I'm very mechanically challenged.  (Damn near mechanically crippled.)  Would another swap be significantly cheaper and still have a similar punch?||||reg||1
Z||000003||87PegasusGT||06-01-2000||03:58 PM||||Unless you have money to blow, go 3.4. It is fun to drive and no kit is required. Any competant mechanic can do the swap and my total bill including the engine came to about $2600 paying the mechanic and including a low mileage engine. Because it still has potential for turbos etc, this was my choice.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87ECO5||06-01-2000||04:02 PM||||What's Ed Parks charging these days for a 3.4 conversion?||||reg||5
Z||000005||Ditz88GT||06-01-2000||05:26 PM||||Do you just swap the block and use the old heads and intake? Or is the convertions a total engine swap?  I thought about this convertion, but I would like to keep the nice fiero intake.  Also, what's the story with the injectors...can you just use the ones from the 3.4?||||reg||5
Z||000006||Raydar||06-01-2000||06:58 PM||||The 3.4 and the Fiero 2.8 heads are identical, right down to the valve sizes. The 2.8 intake system will bolt right on. The 3.4 injectors are recommended. 5.0 TPI injectors are, as I understand it, a direct replacement, too. May be cheaper, since TPI 5.0s are everywhere. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||06-01-2000||10:21 PM||||87pegasusGt, how did your swap wind up costing so much?  I thought you got your engine for cheap?<P>I'm thinking that if I get the engine for around $600 (if 3.4); add $200 for the flywheel, $400 for Fiero Shops clutch, and $500 for some headers, $200 for mill work and 10% for misc things... well, that is $2100, isn't it?  Plus exhaust... another $300, or so.  Plus fluids... $100, using the quality stuff.<P>So that brings the total to $2500........<P>Never mind.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||06-01-2000||10:48 PM||||Raydar, I wouldn't be so sure about ALL 3.4 engines having the HO heads with the larger valves like the Fiero has. I've heard of a few cases where the had the regular heads with the smaller valves. You also never know if the heads have been replaced or not. GM had a habit of switching parts around if they ran out of one, to keep production going.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||06-01-2000||10:54 PM||||sounds like a <B>old</B> ford trick to me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||06-01-2000||11:15 PM||||Batboy, you're right. <BR>What I've heard is that the HO crate motor and the Camaro / Firebird 3.4 all have the "big valve" cast iron heads. Not sure what comes on the other 3.4 applications. If you get one from a rebuilder, it might pay to check, or at least specify what you want. ||||reg||1
